Core principle
  
    - IP address → gets you to the server; port → tells it which service you want; protocol → defines the rules of communication
    - Runs mostly over the TCP/IP model
  
  Key ports
  
    - FTP (file transfer) – port 20/21
    - SSH (encrypted remote access) – port 22
    - SMTP (sending email) – port 25
    - DNS (domain name → IP address) – port 53
    - HTTP (web browsing, unencrypted) – port 80
    - HTTPS (web browsing, encrypted via SSL/TLS) – port 443
    - RDP (Microsoft remote administration) – port 3389
  
  Port security
  
    - Firewalls should only leave the ports open that are actually needed (e.g. close 80/443 if there's no web server)
    - Ports should only be open when in use, not permanently
    - Unencrypted protocols can be encapsulated inside encrypted ones (data stays protected even so)
  
  Authentication & monitoring
  
    - Kerberos as an example of an authentication protocol (identity verification)
    - Intrusion detection/prevention systems learn the normal traffic pattern per port/protocol and 
      flag anomalies (e.g. sudden traffic spikes)
  
  Future trends
  
    - IPv6: much larger address space than IPv4, but longer/less user-friendly
    - Zero Trust: every request is always verified, nothing is assumed secure by default
    - Quantum-safe encryption: encryption via quantum computers, based on quantum physics rather than math — theoretically
      unbreakable


TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) vs UDP (User Datagram Protocol)
  
    - OSI model (Open System Interconnect)
  
  Core principle
  
    - TCP and UDP are the two core transport-layer protocols (Layer 4 of the OSI model) – responsible for actually
      moving packets from one place to another
    - Both balance reliability, speed, and efficiency differently; only one is used per given communication
  
  TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)
  
    - Connection-oriented: three-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK) before any data flows
    - Reliable, ordered, error-checked – via checksums (error detection) and acknowledgments (confirms receipt, otherwise retransmits)
    - Analogy: like a phone call – you dial, wait for an answer, and ask the other person to repeat something you missed
    - Typical uses: web browsing (HTTP/HTTPS), email (SMTP, IMAP, POP3), file transfer (FTP, SFTP)
    - Downside: slower due to error checking and acknowledgments
  
  UDP (User Datagram Protocol)
  
    - Connectionless: no handshake, packets are just sent
    - No delivery guarantee, no retransmission of lost packets → in exchange, lower latency
    - Analogy: like giving a speech to an audience – no one interrupts to ask for a repeat, missed info is simply lost
    - Typical uses: streaming, voice-over-IP, video calls, gaming, DNS lookups, IoT communication
    - Advantage: faster, since there's no error checking or acknowledgments
  
  Comparison
  
  	                TCP	                            UDP
  Connection	      connection-oriented	            connectionless
  Reliability	      high	                          low
  Speed	            slower	                        faster
  Typical for	      web, email, file transfer	      streaming, VoIP, DNS, IoT
  
  Security
  
    - Securing TCP: TLS encryption (e.g. HTTPS), firewall rules allowing only trusted TCP traffic
    - Securing UDP: rate limiting and packet filtering (against DoS attacks), plus DNSSEC for DNS specifically
    - For both: monitoring via intrusion detection systems or traffic-analysis tools like Wireshark


Switches and Routers
  
  
  - Switches connect devices within a LAN
  - Routers connect multiple different LANs/networks together (including your LAN to the internet)
  
  Switches
  
    - Operate at Layer 2 (Data Link Layer) – address and forward traffic using MAC addresses
    - Unmanaged: plug-and-play, no configuration
    - Managed: advanced config options, notably VLAN (Virtual LAN) support and extra security settings
    - Layer 3 switches exist too – they can both switch and route (e.g. a typical home router is technically a layer 3 switch:
      it routes between your LAN and your ISP's network, but also has ports so your own devices can talk to each other)
    - Common use cases: data centers, enterprise networks, VoIP – anywhere there's a LAN; used with VLANs for network
      segmentation and to reduce congestion
  
  Routers
  
    - Operate at Layer 3 (Network Layer) – forward packets using IP addresses, not MAC addresses
    - Connect separate networks together and direct traffic between them
    - Static routing: paths configured manually
    - Dynamic routing: protocols like OSPF, BGP, and EIGRP automatically pick the best path (and can reroute around congestion)
    - Types: home/small-business routers (Wi-Fi, firewall, basic networking), enterprise routers (multi-LAN, multi-site environments),
      core routers (Internet backbone, ISP-scale)
  
  Comparison
  
  	              Switch	                      Router
  OSI Layer	      2 (or 3 for L3 switches)	    3
  Addressing	    MAC address	                  IP address
  Connects	      devices within one LAN	      multiple networks/LANs
  Typical use	    LAN traffic management	      internet access, inter-network routing
  
  Best practices for both
  
    - Change default admin credentials, enable firewall rules
    - Use role-based access control for who can configure the device
    - Segment traffic with VLANs; use QoS to prioritize bandwidth for demanding applications
    - Monitor via SNMP or syslog-based tools; keep firmware updated
    - Back up device configuration to a file so it can be restored after a factory reset


Access Points, Repeaters & Extenders:

    - Access Points (APs): extend an existing wired network into a wireless one
    - Repeaters: boost/rebroadcast an existing Wi-Fi signal to extend coverage
    - Extenders: also extend Wi-Fi coverage, but can use a wired backhaul connection
  
  Access Points
  
    - Connect to the existing wired network via cable, then transmit/receive wirelessly so Wi-Fi devices join the
      same network as the wired one
    - Modern APs support dual-band (2.4/5 GHz) or tri-band (incl. 6 GHz)
    - Managed locally via browser/IP address, or centrally via a cloud service for many APs at once
    - No bandwidth/performance degradation, since the AP connects to the switch like any other wired device
    - Typical use: large offices, enterprise environments, public Wi-Fi hotspots (airports, hotels)
    - Requires a wired (Ethernet) connection to set up
  
  Repeaters
  
    - Receive the existing Wi-Fi signal and rebroadcast it wirelessly only – no wired backhaul option
    - Reduces bandwidth/speed, since the signal has to be received, boosted, and retransmitted
    - Plug-and-play – just connects to the existing Wi-Fi
    - Typical use: small homes/offices with minor dead zones
  
  Extenders
  
    - Can connect back to the original signal via a wired connection ("backhaul") or wirelessly
    - With a wired Ethernet backhaul: extends coverage without reducing speed
    - With a wireless backhaul: functionally the same as a repeater
    - Typical use: larger buildings or areas with significant interference, where running a cable is possible
    
  Comparison
  
  	                      Access Point	           Repeater	            Extender
  Connection to source	  wired	                  wireless only	        wired or wireless
  Speed impact	          none	                  reduces speed	        none (if wired), same as repeater (if wireless)
  Setup	                  requires Ethernet	      plug-and-play	        wired setup if using backhaul
  Typical use	            offices/enterprise	    small homes/offices	  larger offices, weak-signal areas
  
  Best practices
    
    - Place the primary Wi-Fi source as centrally as possible
    - Always secure the wireless signal with a password (not needed for wired backhaul connections)
    - Change default device passwords/credentials – defaults are often weak and well-known
    - Keep firmware updated
    - Monitor device logs/configuration to catch unauthorized changes and see which devices are connecting


Patch Panels

  Core function

    - Patch panels are centralized connection points for network cables – every office/room's cable run terminates there, simplifying
      cable management and troubleshooting
    - The patch panel is essentially "the other end" of the cable from each office; short cables then connect the patch panel to the
      switches, so all devices can communicate
  
  Types

    - Copper patch panels: standard Ethernet/copper wiring, terminated with RJ45 connectors on the front
    - Fiber optic patch panels: same concept, using fiber cabling instead
    - Shielded vs. unshielded: shielded is only needed where interference is a concern (e.g. nearby high-power cables);
      unshielded is standard in most environments

  Benefits
  
    - Simplifies maintenance – one central place to check/fix connections instead of chasing cables through the building
    - Improves flexibility and scalability – more patch panels can be added as the environment grows, as long as cabling
      can physically be run
  
  Installation & best practices
  
    - Label every connection on both ends (e.g. floor + office location) so you always know where the other end is
    - Terminate connections properly and plug each patch panel port into its associated switch
    - Keep cables tied together neatly, avoid kinks (can cause breaks), leave a bit of slack, and consider color-coding by section
  
  Use cases
  
    - Common in large data centers, enterprise networks, and office buildings with enough connections to justify one
    - Occasionally used in home networking too (e.g. new-build homes wired with Ethernet in every room), though Wi-Fi has made
      this less common there


Firewalls

  Core function
  
    - A firewall is a security barrier between a trusted network (home/office) and an untrusted one (typically the internet,
      or any outside network)
    - Filters incoming/outgoing traffic based on predefined rules, deciding what's allowed and what's blocked
    - Main purpose: prevent unauthorized access, malware infiltration, and data breaches
  
  Inspection methods (basic → advanced)
  
    - Packet filtering: inspects at the network layer, allows/blocks based on IP address and port – simple, fast, easy to set up,
      but limited security
    - Stateful inspection: only allows traffic from an already-established, authenticated session – better security than plain packet
      filtering
    - Next-generation firewall (NGFW): built-in intrusion detection/prevention, deep packet inspection (examines actual packet
      contents, not just IP/port), advanced malware filtering using known signatures – used in larger enterprise environments
  
  Deployment types
  
    - Network-based: a physical hardware device (e.g. rack-mounted, alongside routers/switches), protects the entire network
    - Host-based: software installed on an individual computer, usually protects just that one system (though it can protect a
      network if placed at the network edge)
    - Cloud-based (Firewall as a Service / FWaaS): subscription service configured through a browser, protects systems behind it
      (typically cloud-based systems)
  
  Best practices
  
    - Principle of least privilege: limit who can access the firewall device/config software
    - Review and adjust rules regularly, since new applications/services keep appearing
    - Enable intrusion detection/prevention if available (NGFW models)
  
  Use cases
  
    - Enterprise networks: virtually always have a firewall — without one, outside access would be wide open
    - Home/small business: home routers include basic firewall functionality by default (e.g. blocking unsolicited inbound
      connections, unless you explicitly configure remote access)
    - Cloud/remote work: firewall rules need to be configured to allow legitimate remote connections (e.g. admins accessing cloud
      VMs, employees connecting back to the office) while still blocking everything else
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
